For many university-based HEP groups, the path to becoming a production-ready WLCG Tier-3 center can seem daunting, often constrained by limited budgets, small teams, and a steep learning curve for grid middleware. This poster presents the evolution of the NNU HEP Farm not just as a site report, but as a practical case study and blueprint for other groups embarking on a similar journey.
We share our experience transforming a modest local cluster—built on commodity hardware with 768 HTCondor-managed cores and 580TB of NFS storage—into a system designed for seamless grid interoperability. We detail our cost-conscious performance solutions, such as implementing SSD-based LVM caching to overcome HDD I/O bottlenecks for metadata-heavy HEP workloads. Crucially, we outline our step-by-step roadmap for grid adoption:
Network Strategy: Evaluating dedicated lines for HTCondor-CE;
Storage Evolution: The rationale and plan for migrating from NFS to an EOS-based storage element;
Security & Automation: Enforcing robust Grid Certificate/CRL management and adopting Ansible for fully reproducible deployment.
By presenting both our experiences and the challenges we navigated, we aim to provide a clear, actionable template for other university groups to build, optimize, and integrate their own sustainable HEP computing resources ahead of the HL-LHC era.

